  "study_summary": {
    "objective": "Find the central carbon pathways to engineer so A. oryzae becomes a high-yield host for heterologous terpene (natural product) production.",
    "experimental_design": "Wild-type RIB40 was grown in MPY liquid medium (2% maltose) at 30 °C and sampled daily from day 1 to day 5 (3 replicates per day) for RNA-seq and metabolomics.",
    "main_finding": "Maltose was used up within day 1 and growth became stationary after ~3 days; the time course pointed to ethanol fermentation, cytosolic acetyl-CoA from citrate and the mevalonate pathway as targets, and a strain with 13 such edits raised terpene titres up to ~65-fold."
  },
